===============The Diamond Problem
The Diamond Problem arises in multiple inheritance when a class inherits from two classes that both inherit from a common base class. 
This leads to ambiguity because the members of the common base class are inherited twice.

Diamond Problem code-->>
 class D inherits from both class B and class C, and both of these inherit from class A. This means that class D contains two copies of A's 
members (e.g., x). When we try to access x in main(), the compiler throws an ambiguity error because it
cannot determine which x to use: the one inherited through B or the one inherited through C.

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class A{
    public:
        int x;
    A(){
        int x=44;
    }    
    
};

class B:public A{
    //inherits from class A
};

class C:public A{
    //inherits from class A
};

class D:public B,public C{
//class D inherits from both class B and C
    //class D contains 2 copies of A's members x
};
int main() {
    // Write C++ code here
    D obj;
    cout<<obj.x<<" "<<endl;
    
    return 0;
}
