#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class A{
    public:int x;
    A(){x=896;}      
};

class B:virtual public A{
    //inherits from class A
};

class C:virtual public A{
    //inherits from class A
};

class D : public B,public C {};
//class D inherits from both class B and C
    //class D contains 2 copies of A's members x
//To resolve the Diamond Problem, C++ provides the virtual keyword. When a class is inherited virtually, only one copy of the
//base class's members is shared across all derived classes.
int main() {
    // Write C++ code here
    D obj;
    cout<<obj.x<<" "<<endl;    
    return 0;
}
In this code, the virtual keyword ensures that there is only one copy of A's members in class D. When we access obj.x, 
it is clear which x is being referred to, and the ambiguity is resolved.
============
Multiple Inheritance allows a class to inherit from two or more base classes.
It introduces the Diamond Problem, where ambiguity arises due to multiple copies of the same base class members.
The virtual keyword resolves this problem by ensuring that only one copy of the base class's members is shared.
